# ===========================================================================
# MC Nexus — Next.js frontend
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and adjust. `.env.local` is gitignored; this template
# is committed, so it must never contain a real credential.
# ===========================================================================

# Base URL of the Express API in `server/`. Public by design — it is a URL
# the browser has to call anyway.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000


# ===========================================================================
# Meta / Instagram credentials do NOT belong in this file
# ===========================================================================
# They live in `server/.env`, because the Express API is what talks to Meta —
# it holds the OAuth callback, the token exchange and the sync scheduler.
#
# Anything defined here is only readable by Next.js, and anything Next.js
# needs in the browser must be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which Next.js inlines
# verbatim into the client bundle. So a Meta secret placed here is either
# unread (no prefix, and no Next.js route handler exists to read it) or
# published to every visitor (with the prefix). Neither is what you want.
#
# Concretely: the app secret, any access token, and the webhook verify token
# all stay in server/.env — with or without a public prefix. (Their literal
# names are deliberately not written out here, so that a careless grep or
# copy-paste out of this file cannot turn into a live declaration.)
#
# The server refuses to stay quiet about this: `assertNoPublicSecrets()` in
# server/src/services/integrations/meta-config.ts logs a loud warning at boot
# if any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable looks like a secret.
#
# See server/.env.example for the full Meta configuration.
